\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{An annulus that excludes the focus}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{A compact region in the plane that contains no
[equilibrium](fnip-0014), and that the flow does not leave, contains
a [closed orbit](fnip-0052). The forward orbit of any state in the
region stays in the region, so it has an accumulation point. The
[omega-limit set](fnip-0052) of that state is a nonempty compact
subset of the region and contains no equilibrium, and
\ref{fnip-0054} says that this set is a closed orbit.}

\p{\citet{Exercise 1.8.2, §1.8, p. 44}{guckenheimer1983nonlinear}
applies the theorem to two nested closed curves. The flow crosses the
outer curve inward and the inner curve outward, and the van der Pol
oscillator then has a closed orbit in the annulus. The same page
treats a uniqueness argument as a further step.}

\p{For the Morris–Lecar velocity of \ref{fnip-0005} at
#{I_{\mathrm{app}}=100}, take the rectangle #{R} given by
#{V\in[-70,80]} and #{n\in[0,1]}. On #{n=0},
#{\dot n=\phi\, n_{\infty}(V)/\tau_{n}(V)}. For every finite #{V}
one has #{0<n_{\infty}(V)<1}, because #{\tanh} takes values in
#{(-1,1)}, and #{\tau_{n}(V)>0}, so #{\dot n>0}. On #{n=1},
#{\dot n=\phi\,(n_{\infty}(V)-1)/\tau_{n}(V)<0}. On the sides
#{V=80} and #{V=-70}, with #{n\in[0,1]}, the voltage component
points into #{R}. The velocity points into #{R} along
the boundary of #{R}.}

\p{The inner boundary is a closed curve around the equilibrium
#{(V_{*},n_{*})} of \ref{fnip-0017}. On this curve the velocity
points out of the enclosed disk, and the equilibrium lies in the
open disk. The closed region between the curve and the boundary of
#{R} is compact, the velocity points into it, and the equilibrium
of \ref{fnip-0017} lies outside it, so the region contains a closed
orbit.}

\p{The eigenvalues of \ref{fnip-0037} have positive real part, and the
homeomorphism of \ref{fnip-0043} carries that local departure to the
nonlinear flow, so this [focus](fnip-0035) lies in the
[omega-limit set](fnip-0052) of no other state. When a compact region
that the flow does not leave has this focus as its only equilibrium
and contains another state, the [omega-limit set](fnip-0052) of that
state contains no equilibrium, and \ref{fnip-0054} supplies a
[closed orbit](fnip-0052) in the region. The region drawn for this
current is the annulus of the exercise.}

\p{If the region contains an equilibrium, the omega-limit set may be a
closed orbit, or a union of equilibria and orbits that connect them.
\citet{§1.8, p. 45}{guckenheimer1983nonlinear} records these
possibilities for a planar flow. The argument uses the plane.}
